<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What the Extension Manager and extensions.libreoffice.org read.

  The identifier is the name every other file keys off - OptionsDialog.xcu repeats it,
  and test_wiring.py checks they still agree. It is also what an update replaces: change
  it and existing installs get a second, unrelated extension instead of a new version.

  The version floor needs BOTH elements, and the OpenOffice.org one is a formality.
  LibreOffice reports its OpenOffice.org compatibility as 4.1 and always will, whatever
  release it is - so asking for OpenOffice.org-minimal-version 6.1 makes every
  LibreOffice on earth answer "unsatisfied dependencies" and refuse the package.
  Measured on 26.2. The real floor goes in lo:LibreOffice-minimal-version, which only
  LibreOffice reads.

  Where 6.1 comes from. The hard technical floor is LibreOffice 4.0: that is where the
  bundled Python went from 2.6 to 3.3 and the Python scripting provider became core
  rather than an extension, and this is Python 3 code. Everything else is older than
  that - XProofreader and the grammar-checking API are OOo 3.0.1, uno.invoke and
  uno.Any are documented in the OOo 2.x Python bridge, and nothing used here carries an
  @since tag at all. Nothing in the Python needs more than 3.3 either: no f-strings, no
  annotations, no ordering assumptions. 6.1 is therefore margin, not necessity - it is
  simply the oldest release worth claiming to support, and far newer than anything the
  code actually needs.

  No maximum. None of these APIs has been removed, and the one thing that changed is an
  addition: from LibreOffice 26.8, uno.Any can be passed to a method directly, so
  uno.invoke is no longer needed for the string-list write in laita_settings.py. It is
  not deprecated and still exists in master - this is a simplification waiting to be
  taken, not a break waiting to happen.

  Platform is "all" because nothing here is compiled, and nothing is POSIX: the only
  filesystem path is os.path.expanduser("~/laita-libreoffice.log"), which resolves on
  Windows and macOS too. Untested there, though - see libreoffice/README.md.
-->
<description xmlns="http://openoffice.org/extensions/description/2006"
             xmlns:d="http://openoffice.org/extensions/description/2006"
             xmlns:lo="http://libreoffice.org/extensions/description/2011"
             xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">
  <identifier value="org.lobianco.laita" />
  <version value="0.4.3" />

  <display-name>
    <name lang="en">LAITA - Local AI Text Assistant</name>
  </display-name>
  <extension-description>
    <src lang="en" xlink:href="description/desc_en.txt" />
  </extension-description>
  <icon>
    <default xlink:href="icons/extension_42.png" />
  </icon>

  <publisher>
    <name lang="en" xlink:href="https://github.com/sylvaticus/laita">Antonello Lobianco</name>
  </publisher>

  <!-- Pure Python and configuration: nothing here is compiled for an architecture. -->
  <platform value="all" />
  <dependencies>
    <OpenOffice.org-minimal-version value="4.1" d:name="LibreOffice" />
    <lo:LibreOffice-minimal-version value="6.1" d:name="LibreOffice 6.1" />
  </dependencies>
</description>
